  "query_preview": "..\nTonight: Convection will continue into the early morning hours\nof Friday. The best chances remain over the Blues, Camas\nPrairie, and northern mountains. Most Unstable Cape values \nremain in the 500-1500 J/kg range. DCape values are ranging from\n500-1000. It is enough to produce thunderstorms and lead to fire\nweather concerns for dry lightning and gusty outflow winds \nranging from 40-50 mph. Other smaller impacts include small \nhail and brief heavy rain. \n\nFriday and Saturday: As a trough deepens along the coast, a dry\ncold front will swing through the Inland Northwest. Winds\ncombined with the hot, dry conditions are highlighting the\nperiod for critical fire weather concerns. 850-700mb winds are \nexpected to be in the 25-40kt range. The winds will mix down to \nthe surface during the day and bring sustained winds in the \nupper teens into the 20s with gusts into the 30s. Some \nlocations could have gusts reach into the low 40s. For most \nareas, minimum humidity continue to be in the teens during the \ndaytime hours. Friday concerns will be strongest along the \nCascades, Okanogan Valley, and Western Columbia Basin. For \nSaturday, the concern spreads to most across Eastern Washington \nwith higher winds expected. Sustained will be in the 20s and \ngusts into the upper 30s and 40s. Any new or existing fires \nSaturday will be capable of very rapid fire spread. Smoke models\nare hinting at improving air across the Basin but that is \ndependent on the activity of any new and ongoing fires. High \ntemperatures will be on slight cooling trend but still remain in\nthe upper 80s to low 90s. Overnight lows will be in the 60s.\n\nSunday through Wednesday: Ensembles are in broad agreement for \nthe next upper-level trough to move onshore on Sunday, bringing \ncooler conditions, continued breezy winds, and a chance for \nlight preciptiation along the Cascade crest. The Gulf of Alaska \nwill remain active through at least the middle of next week with\nan amplified four corners high into the northern Rockies. \nOnshore flow into western Washington will continue with breezy \nand dry afternoon winds across the Inland Northwest. Highs will\nbe in the 80s to low 90s. Overnight lows will be in the 50s to\nlow 60s. /JDC",
